From c30860a311f401e3047857211d090b2decae19b2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SovereignOS Build <build@sovereignos.local>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 02:23:15 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 86/89] sovereignos: keep the bouncer up across an authorized
 switch

Two previous attempts at the reported self-swiping unlock both failed
because they suppressed animations underneath the keyguard rather than
the keyguard's own state machine. A lossless 64MiB capture of a real
duress unlock shows what actually renders:

  RESET
  STARTED  PRIMARY_BOUNCER -> LOCKSCREEN  by FromPrimaryBouncerTransitionInteractor
  FINISHED PRIMARY_BOUNCER -> LOCKSCREEN                       (471ms)
  keyguardGoingAway requested for userId: 10
  STARTED  LOCKSCREEN -> GONE  by FromLockscreenTransitionInteractor
  FINISHED LOCKSCREEN -> GONE                                  (416ms)

The incoming user's lock screen is animated into view and then animated
away again. Both transitions are visible, and together they are the
swipe. An ordinary unlock on this same device goes PRIMARY_BOUNCER ->
GONE in a single step and never presents a lock screen at all --
captured directly for comparison, not assumed.

The cause is resetStateLocked() collapsing the bouncer as part of the
switch. That drops primaryBouncerShowing, and
FromPrimaryBouncerTransitionInteractor#listenForPrimaryBouncerNotShowing
turns exactly that into a transition to LOCKSCREEN. Everything after it
is forced: once a lock screen is showing, dismissing it needs a second
transition.

Uses resetStateLocked's existing hideBouncer parameter to leave the
bouncer up for an authorized switch, so the state machine stays on the
single-step PRIMARY_BOUNCER -> GONE path. The bouncer covers the screen
at least as completely as the lock screen it replaces, so nothing of the
outgoing user's session is exposed, and it is the surface the credential
was just typed into.

The grant moves to handleBeforeUserSwitching since the decision must be
made there; switch-complete now reads that decision instead of redeeming
a second time. Ordinary secure-user switches are untouched and still
collapse their bouncer.
---
 .../keyguard/KeyguardViewMediator.java        | 77 ++++++++++++-------
 1 file changed, 49 insertions(+), 28 deletions(-)

diff --git a/packages/SystemUI/src/com/android/systemui/keyguard/KeyguardViewMediator.java b/packages/SystemUI/src/com/android/systemui/keyguard/KeyguardViewMediator.java
index 1f0d955ce545..00654365cdca 100644
--- a/packages/SystemUI/src/com/android/systemui/keyguard/KeyguardViewMediator.java
+++ b/packages/SystemUI/src/com/android/systemui/keyguard/KeyguardViewMediator.java
@@ -496,26 +496,25 @@ public class KeyguardViewMediator implements CoreStartable,
     private final Object mDismissToken = new Object();
 
     /**
-     * SovereignOS: set when handleUserSwitchComplete's authorized branch is the thing that requested
-     * the upcoming keyguard dismissal, so mKeyguardGoingAwayRunnable can suppress the unlock
-     * window animation for it (see KEYGUARD_GOING_AWAY_FLAG_NO_WINDOW_ANIMATIONS there).
+     * SovereignOS: set in handleBeforeUserSwitching when the incoming switch holds a still-valid
+     * decoy-switch authorization, by redeeming that mint's bouncer-skip grant. It drives three
+     * things, in order: the bouncer is left up across the switch instead of being collapsed into
+     * the incoming user's lock screen (handleBeforeUserSwitching), the automatic bouncer re-arm is
+     * skipped and the dismissal is requested immediately (handleUserSwitchComplete), and the
+     * canned unlock animation is suppressed (KeyguardUnlockAnimationController#
+     * playCannedUnlockAnimation, which consumes it via consumeAuthorizedSwitchDismiss).
      *
-     * Without this the keyguard, which is necessarily still showing across the switch (hiding it
-     * any earlier would expose the outgoing user's own screen -- switchToUserSilently suppresses
-     * the UserSwitchingDialog, so nothing covers the transition), animates itself away once the
-     * incoming user's home is behind it. That reads on screen as the lock screen swiping itself
-     * up, which an ordinary same-user unlock never does, so it is a tell in exactly the situation
-     * this feature exists for. Cutting instead of animating is the closest achievable match.
+     * The first of those is what makes the switch look like an ordinary unlock. Collapsing the
+     * bouncer drives PRIMARY_BOUNCER -> LOCKSCREEN, so the incoming user's lock screen animates
+     * into view and then has to be animated away again by a second LOCKSCREEN -> GONE transition
+     * -- 471ms plus 416ms, measured on hardware, and that pair is what reads as the device swiping
+     * itself up. Leaving the bouncer up keeps the state machine on PRIMARY_BOUNCER -> GONE, the
+     * single-step path an ordinary unlock takes.
      *
-     * Read and written only on the mHandler/mediator thread (handleUserSwitchComplete,
-     * handleBeforeUserSwitching and handleHide are all dispatched from it), and cleared on an
-     * intervening switch alongside the mDismissToken callbacks it belongs to.
-     *
-     * It is set immediately before a 0ms-delayed dismiss on that same thread, so in practice the
-     * next going-away is that dismiss. It is not proven impossible for some other unlock to reach
-     * mKeyguardGoingAwayRunnable first and consume it; the consequence if that ever happened is
-     * one unlock rendered without its window animation, which is cosmetic and carries no
-     * authorization -- the flag gates animation only, never whether a dismissal is permitted.
+     * Read and written only on the mHandler/mediator thread. Reassigned on every switch, so a
+     * superseded one cannot leave a stale mark behind. It gates presentation only -- it never
+     * decides whether a dismissal is permitted, which UserController's grants settle before it is
+     * ever set, and it can only ever be set by redeeming a real single-use grant.
      */
     private boolean mAuthorizedSwitchDismissPending;
 
@@ -722,9 +721,13 @@ public class KeyguardViewMediator implements CoreStartable,
         Log.d(TAG, String.format("onBeforeUserSwitching %d", userId));
         synchronized (KeyguardViewMediator.this) {
             mHandler.removeCallbacksAndMessages(mDismissToken);
-            // SovereignOS: cancelled alongside the dismissal it belongs to, so a superseded switch
-            // cannot carry its animation suppression into an unrelated later unlock.
-            mAuthorizedSwitchDismissPending = false;
+            // SovereignOS: the bouncer-skip grant is redeemed here, the earliest SystemUI-side point
+            // in the switch, rather than at handleUserSwitchComplete as it used to be, because
+            // the decision it gates has to be made now: whether the bouncer stays up across the
+            // switch (see the resetStateLocked call below). Assigned rather than merely cleared,
+            // so a switch that earns no grant also resets any stale mark from a superseded one.
+            mAuthorizedSwitchDismissPending = mLockPatternUtils.isSecure(userId)
+                    && mLockPatternUtils.consumeDecoySwitchAuthorizationForBouncerSkip(userId);
             mHandler.removeMessages(START_KEYGUARD_EXIT_ANIM);
             mHandler.removeMessages(DISMISS);
             mHandler.removeMessages(HIDE);
@@ -747,7 +750,24 @@ public class KeyguardViewMediator implements CoreStartable,
                     doKeyguardLocked(null);
                 }
             } else {
-                resetStateLocked();
+                // SovereignOS: on an authorized switch the bouncer is deliberately left up across the
+                // switch instead of being collapsed into the incoming user's lock screen.
+                //
+                // Hiding it drops primaryBouncerShowing, which
+                // FromPrimaryBouncerTransitionInteractor#listenForPrimaryBouncerNotShowing turns
+                // into a PRIMARY_BOUNCER -> LOCKSCREEN transition; the dismissal that follows then
+                // has to run a second, LOCKSCREEN -> GONE transition to get rid of what was just
+                // shown. Confirmed on hardware: 471ms of lock screen animating in, a pause, then
+                // 416ms of it animating away -- which is what reads as the device swiping itself
+                // up. An ordinary unlock goes PRIMARY_BOUNCER -> GONE in one step and never
+                // presents a lock screen at all, which is exactly what leaving the bouncer up
+                // reproduces here.
+                //
+                // The bouncer covers the screen at least as completely as the lock screen it
+                // replaces, so nothing of the outgoing user's session is exposed by this; it is
+                // also the surface the credential was just typed into, so it is what the user is
+                // already looking at.
+                resetStateLocked(/* hideBouncer= */ !mAuthorizedSwitchDismissPending);
             }
             resultCallback.run();
         }
@@ -810,7 +830,10 @@ public class KeyguardViewMediator implements CoreStartable,
             // every other secure-user switch (including any switch this authorization was never
             // minted for) is unaffected and still re-arms the bouncer as before.
             if (mLockPatternUtils.isSecure(userId)) {
-                if (!mLockPatternUtils.consumeDecoySwitchAuthorizationForBouncerSkip(userId)) {
+                // SovereignOS: the grant was already redeemed in handleBeforeUserSwitching, which had
+                // to know whether this switch was authorized before it could decide to leave the
+                // bouncer up. This reads that decision rather than redeeming a second time.
+                if (!mAuthorizedSwitchDismissPending) {
                     // We are calling dismiss with a delay as there are race conditions in some
                     // scenarios caused by async layout listeners
                     mHandler.postDelayed(
@@ -841,11 +864,9 @@ public class KeyguardViewMediator implements CoreStartable,
                     // mDismissToken) above) if an intervening switch supersedes it before this
                     // runs.
                     //
-                    // Marked so mKeyguardGoingAwayRunnable suppresses the unlock window animation
-                    // for this specific dismissal: the keyguard has been showing continuously
-                    // across the switch, so animating it away here renders as the lock screen
-                    // swiping itself up -- see mAuthorizedSwitchDismissPending.
-                    mAuthorizedSwitchDismissPending = true;
+                    // The mark set in handleBeforeUserSwitching stays pending past this point:
+                    // it is consumed where the animation that would otherwise render is actually
+                    // selected, in KeyguardUnlockAnimationController#playCannedUnlockAnimation.
                     mHandler.postDelayed(
                             () -> dismiss(null /* callback */, null /* message */), mDismissToken,
                             0);
-- 
